# Rule: web (Nuxt 4, Vue 3 `<script setup>`, TypeScript), in `<%webDir%>/`

## Layout
```
app/pages/          routes (one file per route), rendered on the server for each request
app/components/     presentational components: props in, markup out, no data fetching
app/utils/          pure helpers, auto-imported
app/assets/css/     global styles
app/__tests__/      Vitest specs for everything under app/
<%#hasBackend%>
server/api/         HTTP routes the page asks (`hello.get.ts` is `GET /api/hello`)
server/utils/       typed API client (mirrors .ai/contract/openapi.json), auto-imported by server code
<%/hasBackend%>
<%^hasBackend%>
server/             HTTP routes (`server/api/<name>.get.ts`) and server-only helpers (`server/utils/`), when the app needs them
<%/hasBackend%>
server/__tests__/   Vitest specs for everything under server/
```

## Conventions
- TypeScript `strict` (+ `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`), `<script setup lang="ts">` everywhere. No `any`; narrow `unknown` with type guards at the boundary.
<%#hasBackend%>
- All backend access goes through `server/utils/api.ts`, called only from `server/api/*` routes. Pages and components never call the backend, and never `fetch` it: they use `useFetch("/api/...")`. Responses are validated against the contract shape before use.
- `API_URL` is a RUNTIME variable (read on the server for each request). Never bake environment-specific URLs into the build (not in `nuxt.config.ts`, not in `runtimeConfig` defaults): one image runs in every environment.
<%/hasBackend%>
<%^hasBackend%>
- This app has no separate backend. Server-side work lives in `server/api/<name>.get.ts` routes and calls plain functions from `server/utils/`. Pages and components never call `fetch` directly: they use `useFetch("/api/...")`.
- Treat everything that comes in (query strings, request bodies, cookies) as `unknown` and validate it at the boundary before use.
- Configuration is read from environment variables at request time, never baked into the build: one image runs in every environment.
<%/hasBackend%>
- Every data-driven view models all its states explicitly: loading, empty, error, success. Errors are shown with `role="alert"`, never swallowed.
- Keep pages thin: a page fetches and decides which component to show; components only render. Put logic in `app/utils/` as plain functions.
- Accessibility: semantic elements, labelled controls, visible focus, sufficient contrast. Don't convey state by colour alone.
- Lint with `eslint --max-warnings 0` (`@nuxt/eslint`, flat config in `eslint.config.mjs`); fix the code instead of adding `eslint-disable`.
- `.nuxt/` and `.output/` are generated: never edit or commit them. `npm run lint` and `npm run type-check` regenerate `.nuxt/` first.

## Tests (Vitest in a Nuxt environment, coverage floor <%coverageWeb%>%)
- Test behaviour through the rendered output (`mountSuspended`, then `get("h1")` / `[role=alert]`), not implementation details.
<%#hasBackend%>
- The page asks `/api/hello`: replace that route with `registerEndpoint("/api/hello", ...)` and call `clearNuxtData()` between specs. Cover the success and the error state.
- Server code is plain TypeScript: import it with a relative path and pass a fake `fetch` into the client. Cover each state of each component and each error path of `server/utils/api.ts`.

## Contract
When the backend contract changes, update `server/utils/api.ts` types and tests in the same PR.
<%/hasBackend%>
<%^hasBackend%>
- Pass the address to a page with `mountSuspended(Page, { route: "/?name=Ada" })`.
- Test pure logic in `app/utils/` directly, without rendering. Cover each state of each component and each rejected input.
<%/hasBackend%>
